G.  LITERATURE FINDING -- energy closure of VEF2010 Appendix A
==============================================================================
   The paper states the last column is 'a test of energy
   conservation'.  Summing the five deposition columns plus the CMB
   column at x_e = 1e-4:
     z        E_in         closure
     10       1e+06        1.0002
     10       1e+07        0.9614
     10       1e+08        0.8827
     10       1e+09        0.9863
     10       1e+10        0.9986
     10       1e+11        0.9999
     10       1e+12        1.0000
     30       1e+06        1.0001
     30       1e+07        0.8322
     30       1e+08        0.9242
     30       1e+09        0.9919
     30       1e+10        0.9992
     30       1e+11        0.9999
     30       1e+12        1.0000
     50       1e+06        1.0000
     50       1e+07        0.7903
     50       1e+08        0.9387
     50       1e+09        0.9936
     50       1e+10        0.9994
     50       1e+11        0.9999
     50       1e+12        1.0000

   Closure is 1.0000-1.0002 at E_in = 1 MeV, which confirms the
   column identification, but falls to 0.77-0.94 at 10-100 MeV.
   The missing energy is inverse-Compton photons between 10.2 eV
   and 10 keV, which fall in no tabulated column.  This is a
   property of the published tables, and it limits how tightly
   engine B constrains engine A in that decade.

==============================================================================
H.  LITERATURE FINDING -- the CMB in Tueros et al. (2014)
==============================================================================
   Tueros et al. (2014), Sect. 2, verbatim:
     "The CMB has been considered monoenergetic with photon energy
      E_CMB = 3.75 x 10^-4 (1 + z) eV, and a photon density
      u_CMB = 0.05 (1 + z)^3 cm^-3."

   Measured CMB at z = 0 (T = 2.7255 K, Fixsen 2009), computed here:
     kT              = 2.3487e-04 eV
     n_gamma         = 410.7268 cm^-3   (standard value 410.7)
     u_gamma         = 2.6057e-01 eV cm^-3
     <E> = u/n       = 6.3441e-04 eV  = 2.701 kT

   Their numbers at z = 0:
     E_CMB           = 3.750e-04 eV   -> 1.69 x below <E>
     u_CMB           = 5.000e-02 cm^-3 -> 8214.5 x below n_gamma
     implied energy density = 1.875e-05 eV cm^-3 -> 7.196e-05 x below u_gamma

   Read instead as an ENERGY density of 0.05 eV cm^-3, the
   shortfall would be a factor 5.21.
   Either reading under-estimates the inverse-Compton energy loss
   rate, which scales linearly with u_CMB.  That is the direction
   needed to explain why their ionization yields exceed engine A's
   (block J), and it is stated here as an observation about the
   published parameters, not as a re-derivation of their result.

